1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,727
Night.

2
00:00:00,923 --> 00:00:02,184
The Arabian Sea.

3
00:00:02,575 --> 00:00:06,993
Somewhere under these waves, an Indian submarine is running out of battery.

4
00:00:07,442 --> 00:00:09,373
Imagine you're in its control room.

5
00:00:09,732 --> 00:00:11,250
Nobody raises a voice.

6
00:00:11,503 --> 00:00:13,736
The captain gives one quiet order.

7
00:00:14,138 --> 00:00:15,189
Raise the snorkel.

8
00:00:15,556 --> 00:00:18,565
A thin steel mast rises through black water.

9
00:00:18,807 --> 00:00:20,740
The diesel engines roar to life.

10
00:00:20,934 --> 00:00:26,122
And for the next few hours, you are easier to find than at almost any other time on your patrol.

11
00:00:26,433 --> 00:00:28,917
You're inside a machine built to disappear.

12
00:00:29,167 --> 00:00:31,800
This is the most dangerous part of its life.

13
00:00:32,035 --> 00:00:34,117
And it has to do it every few days.

14
00:00:34,330 --> 00:00:38,163
So why would anyone build a hiding machine that has to show itself?

15
00:00:38,577 --> 00:00:39,536
There is a cure.

16
00:00:39,795 --> 00:00:41,249
You've probably heard its name.

17
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:44,406
AIP, air independent propulsion.

18
00:00:44,611 --> 00:00:46,924
Online, they call it the ghost maker.

19
00:00:47,151 --> 00:00:50,509
Our submarines will become invisible, the headlines say.

20
00:00:50,756 --> 00:00:51,803
But here's the catch.

21
00:00:51,959 --> 00:00:56,037
To fit it, India has to cut one of its own submarines in two.

22
00:00:56,278 --> 00:00:57,525
And it gets stranger.

23
00:00:57,721 --> 00:01:00,830
India built this machine, with its own scientists.

24
00:01:00,915 --> 00:01:02,355
It has run on land.

25
00:01:02,544 --> 00:01:06,728
Yet the Navy leaned towards a German design for its next submarines.

26
00:01:06,908 --> 00:01:08,485
So was the Navy wrong?

27
00:01:08,576 --> 00:01:10,828
Or was India's machine not good enough?

28
00:01:11,119 --> 00:01:13,051
The answer is, both were right.

29
00:01:13,196 --> 00:01:15,332
By the end of this film, you'll see why.

30
00:01:15,611 --> 00:01:18,355
Start with the people whose job is to find you.

31
00:01:18,567 --> 00:01:19,951
Now switch sides.

32
00:01:20,081 --> 00:01:20,989
You're the hunter.

33
00:01:21,157 --> 00:01:25,532
You're flying a P-8I of the Indian Navy, hours out over the sea.

34
00:01:25,695 --> 00:01:27,563
You don't look for the submarine itself.

35
00:01:27,822 --> 00:01:29,844
You look for what it leaves behind.

36
00:01:29,993 --> 00:01:30,826
A mast.

37
00:01:31,024 --> 00:01:33,523
The thin white wake it draws on the water.

38
00:01:33,722 --> 00:01:35,133
A dot on your radar.

39
00:01:35,304 --> 00:01:37,131
The noise of a diesel engine.

40
00:01:38,108 --> 00:01:39,095
Now switch back.

41
00:01:39,295 --> 00:01:40,751
You're in the submarine again.

42
00:01:40,942 --> 00:01:43,545
So why would you ever raise that mast?

43
00:01:43,746 --> 00:01:48,921
Because under water, everything you depend on runs on one giant battery.

44
00:01:49,186 --> 00:01:52,759
The motor, the lights, the air plant, the computers.

45
00:01:53,054 --> 00:01:56,209
And the engines that charge your battery need air.

46
00:01:56,566 --> 00:01:58,170
Down there, there is none.

47
00:01:58,433 --> 00:02:00,530
So how long can your battery last?

48
00:02:00,832 --> 00:02:01,710
A few days.

49
00:02:01,952 --> 00:02:07,445
The makers of India's Kalvari class say about four, and only if you move at a crawl.

50
00:02:07,664 --> 00:02:12,695
Then you must come up near the surface, raise your mast, and breathe for hours.

51
00:02:12,969 --> 00:02:15,205
Picture your patrol as a strip of time.

52
00:02:15,472 --> 00:02:17,412
Every few days, a red mark.

53
00:02:17,682 --> 00:02:22,457
Each red mark is a few hours when you can be heard, seen, or caught on radar.

54
00:02:22,744 --> 00:02:25,467
For a submarine navy, that's the whole game, right?

55
00:02:25,685 --> 00:02:26,883
Fewer red marks.

56
00:02:26,889 --> 00:02:28,128
Shorter red marks.

57
00:02:28,327 --> 00:02:30,553
That's exactly what AIP does.

58
00:02:30,718 --> 00:02:33,851
It's a second engine that doesn't need air from outside.

59
00:02:34,143 --> 00:02:36,086
Three main kinds are in use today.

60
00:02:36,263 --> 00:02:39,861
A Stirling engine, a steam turbine, and a fuel cell.

61
00:02:40,062 --> 00:02:41,808
India chose the fuel cell.

62
00:02:41,910 --> 00:02:44,529
And for you, the difference is huge.

63
00:02:44,712 --> 00:02:48,798
About four days on the battery becomes about two weeks under water.

64
00:02:49,044 --> 00:02:52,211
That's DRDO's own figure from its land trials.

65
00:02:52,390 --> 00:02:53,677
So, a ghost?

66
00:02:53,878 --> 00:02:54,586
Not quite.

67
00:02:54,783 --> 00:02:57,008
AIP lets you crawl for weeks.

68
00:02:57,051 --> 00:02:58,344
It doesn't let you run.

69
00:02:58,613 --> 00:03:02,199
Push for speed, and you're back on the battery, draining it fast.

70
00:03:02,424 --> 00:03:05,488
And even when you're quiet, you can still be found.

71
00:03:05,724 --> 00:03:08,412
Active sonar can bounce sound off your hull.

72
00:03:08,592 --> 00:03:11,680
And the moment you raise a mast, you can still be seen.

73
00:03:12,672 --> 00:03:15,657
What it really buys you is fewer red marks.

74
00:03:15,871 --> 00:03:17,997
Fewer moments of being exposed.

75
00:03:18,299 --> 00:03:21,312
At sea, that can decide whether you come home.

76
00:03:21,556 --> 00:03:24,923
Then you might ask, why not just build nuclear submarines?

77
00:03:25,181 --> 00:03:26,493
India does build them.

78
00:03:26,650 --> 00:03:29,987
But they are far bigger, far more costly, and few.

79
00:03:30,269 --> 00:03:36,073
In the shallow waters close to our coast, a small, quiet diesel boat is still the right tool.

80
00:03:36,247 --> 00:03:38,444
So India built its own AIP.

81
00:03:38,674 --> 00:03:40,575
And it built it in its own way.

82
00:03:40,707 --> 00:03:45,338
Germany's fuel cell submarines carry their hydrogen locked inside metal tanks.

83
00:03:45,549 --> 00:03:48,310
India's carries no hydrogen gas at all.

84
00:03:48,466 --> 00:03:51,297
Instead, it carries a dry white powder.

85
00:03:51,468 --> 00:03:53,002
Sodium borohydride.

86
00:03:53,218 --> 00:03:55,825
Add water, and the powder gives off hydrogen.

87
00:03:55,997 --> 00:03:59,713
Right there, inside the submarine, only as much as you need.

88
00:03:59,804 --> 00:04:01,996
How much power is hiding in that powder?

89
00:04:02,135 --> 00:04:07,036
One spoonful, about 7 grams, makes enough electricity to charge your phone.

90
00:04:07,138 --> 00:04:09,432
That's our rough calculation, on paper.

91
00:04:09,625 --> 00:04:11,329
Now picture tonnes of it.

92
00:04:11,472 --> 00:04:13,594
The hydrogen then meets oxygen.

93
00:04:13,722 --> 00:04:17,713
The oxygen is carried as a liquid, kept very, very cold.

94
00:04:17,837 --> 00:04:21,916
The two meet inside a fuel cell, and its heart is an acid.

95
00:04:22,016 --> 00:04:23,211
You won't see a flame.

96
00:04:23,321 --> 00:04:24,358
No explosion.

97
00:04:24,496 --> 00:04:26,778
Just a steady flow of electricity.

98
00:04:26,901 --> 00:04:28,531
And one more thing comes out.

99
00:04:28,665 --> 00:04:29,228
Water.

100
00:04:29,369 --> 00:04:33,062
That water flows back to the powder, to make more hydrogen.

101
00:04:33,174 --> 00:04:36,805
Would you want all this inside a sealed steel tube with you?

102
00:04:36,909 --> 00:04:40,926
In October 2026, India's Defence Secretary said it simply.

103
00:04:40,987 --> 00:04:45,560
These systems need a strict safety culture, with no compromise.

104
00:04:46,206 --> 00:04:49,204
On paper, India's design has a real edge.

105
00:04:49,402 --> 00:04:52,232
There's no tank of hydrogen gas waiting to leak.

106
00:04:52,452 --> 00:04:53,302
And the price?

107
00:04:53,471 --> 00:04:58,431
Weight, and a salty waste liquid that the boat must hold, or pump out to sea.

108
00:04:58,650 --> 00:04:59,464
Powder in.

109
00:04:59,613 --> 00:05:00,371
Power out.

110
00:05:00,534 --> 00:05:02,350
Under water, for weeks.

111
00:05:02,684 --> 00:05:04,196
But there's a problem.

112
00:05:04,437 --> 00:05:07,850
A Kalvari submarine is about 67 metres long.

113
00:05:08,096 --> 00:05:11,632
Walk through it, and you'll find every metre already full.

114
00:05:11,843 --> 00:05:14,077
So where does this whole plant go?

115
00:05:14,328 --> 00:05:16,472
You open the submarine like a book.

116
00:05:16,586 --> 00:05:19,830
At Mazagon Dock in Mumbai, the hull is cut in two.

117
00:05:19,943 --> 00:05:25,732
A brand-new section is built, holding the powder, the oxygen, the fuel cells and the waste.

118
00:05:25,850 --> 00:05:29,234
It slides into the gap, and the two parts are joined around it.

119
00:05:29,292 --> 00:05:32,593
Reports say the boat comes out about 10 metres longer.

120
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:34,317
Picture 10 metres.

121
00:05:34,456 --> 00:05:37,682
That's about four autorickshaws, parked bumper to bumper.

122
00:05:37,788 --> 00:05:40,666
Now, would you trust a hull that has been cut open?

123
00:05:40,753 --> 00:05:47,989
That's why the submarine's original designer, France's Naval Group, watches over the work and signs off the design for safe fitting.

124
00:05:48,138 --> 00:05:50,622
So the plan is ready, and the machine works.

125
00:05:50,789 --> 00:05:52,179
That's what you'd expect, right?

126
00:05:52,338 --> 00:05:53,851
Here's the honest answer.

127
00:05:53,967 --> 00:05:55,280
Does it work on land?

128
00:05:55,376 --> 00:05:55,950
Yes.

129
00:05:55,991 --> 00:06:03,859
On 8 March 2021, DRDO's land prototype was proven, in its endurance mode and its maximum power mode.

130
00:06:03,973 --> 00:06:06,360
But it has never been to sea.

131
00:06:06,978 --> 00:06:09,572
And at sea, the rules are different.

132
00:06:09,909 --> 00:06:16,042
For its next six submarines, the Navy asked for an air independent engine already proven at sea.

133
00:06:16,286 --> 00:06:17,148
Not on shore.

134
00:06:17,366 --> 00:06:18,085
At sea.

135
00:06:18,350 --> 00:06:24,574
Reports say a bid from L and T with Spain's Navantia was turned down for exactly this reason.

136
00:06:24,757 --> 00:06:27,877
Its system had been shown on shore, not at sea.

137
00:06:28,094 --> 00:06:31,771
Germany's fuel cell boats have been at sea for about 20 years.

138
00:06:31,881 --> 00:06:34,498
So the Navy leaned towards a German design.

139
00:06:34,711 --> 00:06:36,873
Does that mean India's machine is bad?

140
00:06:37,089 --> 00:06:37,522
No.

141
00:06:37,729 --> 00:06:40,995
It means nobody has proven our system at sea yet.

142
00:06:41,169 --> 00:06:44,157
Look at it from both sides, and the trap becomes clear.

143
00:06:44,338 --> 00:06:48,721
The Navy can't bet a war submarine on an engine that has never been to sea.

144
00:06:48,895 --> 00:06:53,111
And the engine can't go to sea until a submarine is cut open for it.

145
00:06:53,257 --> 00:06:54,175
Both are right.

146
00:06:54,349 --> 00:06:55,816
That's what makes it a trap.

147
00:06:55,990 --> 00:06:58,020
And there's one more lock on that trap.

148
00:06:58,186 --> 00:07:03,959
A submarine can only be cut open in a big refit, when it leaves service for months of repair.

149
00:07:04,119 --> 00:07:06,698
And that comes once in several years.

150
00:07:06,854 --> 00:07:08,865
So what happened in 2025?

151
00:07:09,151 --> 00:07:12,216
India's first Kalvari boat was in for its refit.

152
00:07:12,335 --> 00:07:14,132
The new engine wasn't ready.

153
00:07:14,256 --> 00:07:16,073
The boat came out without it.

154
00:07:16,265 --> 00:07:17,816
The window closed.

155
00:07:18,033 --> 00:07:21,180
So if you were in charge, how would you break the trap?

156
00:07:21,323 --> 00:07:23,199
You'd start with one submarine.

157
00:07:23,382 --> 00:07:25,878
Reports say it will be INS Khanderi.

158
00:07:26,804 --> 00:07:33,975
This October, the heart of this machine started its next round of tests, at L and T's yard in Hazira, Gujarat.

159
00:07:34,223 --> 00:07:37,896
Next, it gets powered up and tested to the Navy's rules.

160
00:07:38,133 --> 00:07:40,641
Early next year, it goes to Mazagon Dock.

161
00:07:40,827 --> 00:07:46,467
And the Defence Secretary says the fitting comes around the middle of next year, with sea trials to follow.

162
00:07:46,710 --> 00:07:49,651
Designed at DRDO's lab in Ambernath.

163
00:07:49,832 --> 00:07:51,272
Built by L and T.

164
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:53,186
Fitted by Mazagon Dock.

165
00:07:53,390 --> 00:07:58,218
And the plan is to give it to all six Kalvari boats, one refit at a time.

166
00:07:58,469 --> 00:08:00,412
Meanwhile, is the sea waiting?

167
00:08:00,766 --> 00:08:01,233
No.

168
00:08:01,517 --> 00:08:05,049
China's Yuan class submarines already have this technology.

169
00:08:05,305 --> 00:08:12,860
In April 2026, Pakistan commissioned its first Hangor class submarine, which its navy says has AIP too.

170
00:08:13,157 --> 00:08:17,964
So everything you've heard now rests on one boat, and one set of trials.

171
00:08:18,212 --> 00:08:22,343
If those trials work, India will have what no tender can turn down.

172
00:08:22,592 --> 00:08:25,222
Its own AIP, proven at sea.

173
00:08:25,526 --> 00:08:29,536
The trap opens, for this boat and every boat after it.

174
00:08:29,804 --> 00:08:32,358
Now go back to that night in the Arabian Sea.

175
00:08:32,622 --> 00:08:34,094
You're in the control room again.

176
00:08:34,292 --> 00:08:35,370
The battery is low.

177
00:08:35,569 --> 00:08:36,833
You wait for the order.

178
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:39,701
And this time, the mast stays down.

179
00:08:39,971 --> 00:08:43,030
The submarine slips deeper, and keeps going.

180
00:08:43,297 --> 00:08:45,392
But for now, that's only a hope.

181
00:08:45,632 --> 00:08:48,425
If the trials fail, the trap closes again.

182
00:08:48,654 --> 00:08:52,492
That's why the coming year matters more than any headline you'll read.

183
00:08:52,991 --> 00:08:54,579
This story isn't over.

184
00:08:54,822 --> 00:08:58,523
When Khanderi goes back to sea with its new engine, we'll be there.

185
00:08:58,768 --> 00:09:00,672
Subscribe, so you're there too.

186
00:09:01,170 --> 00:09:03,158
Next, we go even deeper.

187
00:09:03,307 --> 00:09:09,801
6,000 metres down, inside a titanium sphere built to carry Indians to the bottom of the ocean.
